Article 6 Where a non-profit school, kindergarten, medical institution, elderly care institution, or similar entity, which is established for public welfare purposes, provides a guarantee, the People's Court shall determine that the guarantee contract is void, except under any of the following circumstances:
(1) When purchasing or leasing (through financial leasing) educational facilities, medical and health facilities, elderly care service facilities, or other public welfare facilities, the seller or lessor retains ownership of the public welfare facility to secure the payment of the price or rent;
(2) Creating a security interest on immovable property, movable property, or property rights other than educational facilities, medical and health facilities, elderly care service facilities, or other public welfare facilities. Where a school, kindergarten, medical institution, elderly care institution, or similar entity registered as a for-profit legal person provides a guarantee, and a party claims that the guarantee contract is void on the grounds that the entity lacks guarantee capacity, the People's Court shall not support such claim.

Article 8 Under any of the following circumstances, where a company claims that it shall not bear guarantee liability on the grounds that it has not adopted a resolution in accordance with the provisions of the Company Law on external guarantees by companies, the People's Court shall not support such claim:
(1) A financial institution issues a letter of guarantee or a guarantee company provides a guarantee;
(2) A company provides a guarantee for its wholly-owned subsidiary in the course of its business operations;
(3) The guarantee contract is signed with the consent of shareholders who individually or jointly hold two-thirds or more of the voting rights on the guarantee matter. The provisions of items (2) and (3) of the preceding paragraph shall not apply where a listed company provides an external guarantee.

Article 16 Where the parties to the main contract agree to repay an old loan with a new loan, and the creditor requests the guarantor of the old loan to assume guarantee liability, the People's Court shall not support such a request. Where the creditor requests the guarantor of the new loan to assume guarantee liability, the matter shall be handled according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the guarantor of the new loan is the same as that of the old loan, the People's Court shall support the request;
(2) If the guarantor of the new loan is different from that of the old loan, or if the old loan has no guarantee but the new loan has a guarantee, the People's Court shall not support the request, unless the creditor has evidence proving that the guarantor of the new loan knew or should have known the fact of repaying the old loan with the new loan at the time of providing the guarantee. Where the parties to the main contract agree to repay an old loan with a new loan, and the security provider of the old loan agrees to continue providing guarantee for the new loan while the registration has not yet been cancelled, and before concluding the new loan contract, creates a security interest in the secured property for another creditor, and the other creditor claims that its security interest has priority over the creditor of the new loan, the People's Court shall not support such a claim.

Article 17 Where the main contract is valid but the guarantee contract provided by a third party is invalid, the People's Court shall determine the guarantor's compensation liability based on different circumstances:
(1) If both the creditor and the guarantor are at fault, the compensation liability borne by the guarantor shall not exceed one-half of the portion that the debtor cannot pay;
(2) If the guarantor is at fault but the creditor is not, the guarantor shall bear compensation liability for the portion that the debtor cannot pay;
(3) If the creditor is at fault but the guarantor is not, the guarantor shall not bear compensation liability. Where the invalidity of the main contract causes the guarantee contract provided by a third party to be invalid, and the guarantor is not at fault, the guarantor shall not bear compensation liability. If the guarantor is at fault, the compensation liability borne by the guarantor shall not exceed one-third of the portion that the debtor cannot pay.

Article 28 In a general suretyship, where the creditor applies for compulsory enforcement against the debtor's property according to law based on an effective legal instrument, the limitation period for the surety debt shall commence in accordance with the following rules:
(1) Where the People's Court renders a ruling to conclude the current enforcement proceedings, or renders a ruling to terminate enforcement pursuant to subparagraphs (3) and (5) of Article 257 of the Civil Procedure Law, the period shall commence from the date on which the ruling is served on the creditor;
(2) Where the People's Court fails to render the ruling referred to in the preceding subparagraph within one year from the date of receiving the application for enforcement, the period shall commence from the date on which one year has elapsed from the date of the People's Court's receipt of the application for enforcement, except where the surety has evidence proving that the debtor still has property available for enforcement. Where the creditor in a general suretyship files a lawsuit or applies for arbitration against the debtor before the expiration of the suretyship period, and the creditor adduces evidence proving the existence of the circumstances specified in the proviso of Article 687, paragraph 2 of the Civil Code, the limitation period for the surety debt shall commence from the date on which the creditor knows or should have known of such circumstances.

Article 45 Where the parties agree that, when the debtor fails to perform an obligation upon maturity or the circumstances agreed upon by the parties for enforcing the security interest occur, the secured party has the right to self-auction or self-sell the secured property and receive priority payment from the proceeds obtained, such agreement shall be valid. If due to reasons attributable to the grantor of the security, the secured party is unable to self-auction or self-sell the secured property, and the secured party requests the grantor of the security to bear the costs incurred as a result, the People's Court shall support such request. Where a party applies to the People's Court for an auction or sale of the secured property in accordance with the provisions of the Civil Procedure Law concerning 'cases of enforcing security interests,' and the respondent requests the dismissal of the application on the ground that the security contract contains an arbitration clause, the People's Court, after review, shall handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the parties have no substantive dispute over the security interest and the conditions for enforcing the security interest have been fulfilled, the People's Court shall rule to permit the auction or sale of the secured property;
(2) If the parties have a partial substantive dispute over the enforcement of the security interest, the People's Court may rule to permit the auction or sale of the secured property with respect to the undisputed part and inform the parties that they may apply for arbitration with respect to the disputed part;
(3) If the parties have a substantive dispute over the enforcement of the security interest, the People's Court shall rule to dismiss the application and inform the parties that they may apply for arbitration with an arbitration institution. Where a creditor exercises a security interest by way of litigation, the debtor and the grantor of the security shall be named as co-defendants.
(2) Mortgage of Immovable Property

Article 54 Where a mortgage contract for movables is concluded but the mortgage is not registered, the effect of the mortgage on movables shall be dealt with according to the following circumstances:
(1) Where the mortgagor transfers the mortgaged property, and the transferee takes possession of the mortgaged property, if the mortgagee requests the transferee to exercise the mortgage right, the People's Court shall not support such request, except where the mortgagee can adduce evidence proving that the transferee knew or should have known that a mortgage contract had been concluded;
(2) Where the mortgagor leases the mortgaged property to another person and transfers possession, and the mortgagee exercises the mortgage right, the lease relationship shall not be affected, except where the mortgagee can adduce evidence proving that the lessee knew or should have known that a mortgage contract had been concluded;
(3) Where another creditor of the mortgagor applies to the People's Court for preservation or execution of the mortgaged property, and the People's Court has made a property preservation ruling or taken enforcement measures, if the mortgagee claims priority satisfaction from the mortgaged property, the People's Court shall not support such claim;
(4) Where the mortgagor becomes bankrupt, and the mortgagee claims priority satisfaction from the mortgaged property, the People's Court shall not support such claim.

Article 56 Where a buyer acquires, in the ordinary course of business of the seller, by paying a reasonable price, a movable on which a security interest has been established, and the holder of the security interest requests priority satisfaction from such movable, the People's Court shall not support such request, except in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The quantity of goods purchased significantly exceeds that of an ordinary buyer;
(2) The purchase is of the seller's production equipment;
(3) The purpose of concluding the sales contract is to guarantee the performance of a debt by the seller or a third party;
(4) A direct or indirect control relationship exists between the buyer and the seller;
(5) Other circumstances where the buyer should have searched the mortgage registration but failed to do so. The term 'ordinary course of business of the seller' as set forth in the preceding paragraph means that the seller's business activities fall within the business scope explicitly stated in its business license, and the seller continuously sells the same type of goods. The term 'holder of the security interest' as set forth in the preceding paragraph refers to a mortgagee who has undergone registration, a seller in a sale with retention of title, or a lessor in a financial lease contract.

Article 57 After a security provider has established a floating mortgage on movables and registered such mortgage, if the security provider subsequently acquires or leases under a financial lease arrangement new movables, and the following right holders, for the purpose of securing the realization of the purchase price claim or the rent, conclude a security contract and undergo registration within ten days after the delivery of such movables, claiming that their rights are prioritized over the previously established floating mortgage, the People's Court shall support such claim:
(1) The seller who establishes a mortgage on or retains title to such movables;
(2) A creditor who, to provide financing for the payment of the purchase price, establishes a mortgage on such movables;
(3) The lessor who leases such movables under a financial lease arrangement. Where a buyer acquires movables but does not pay the full purchase price, or a lessee takes possession of the leased property under a financial lease arrangement but does not pay the full rent, and subsequently establishes a security interest on the subject matter in favor of another person, if the right holders listed in the preceding paragraph, for the purpose of securing the realization of the purchase price claim or the rent, conclude a security contract and undergo registration within ten days after the delivery of such movables, claiming that their rights are prioritized over the security interest established by the buyer in favor of another person, the People's Court shall support such claim. Where multiple purchase money security interests exist on the same movable, the People's Court shall determine the order of satisfaction according to the chronological order of registration.

Article 65 In a financial leasing contract, where the lessee fails to pay the rent as agreed and, after a demand for payment, still fails to pay within a reasonable period, if the lessor requests the lessee to pay all remaining rent and to be satisfied from the proceeds of an auction or sale of the leased property, the People's Court shall support such request; if a party requests a reference to the relevant provisions of the Civil Procedure Law on "cases for the realization of security interests" for the payment of rent from the proceeds of an auction or sale of the leased property, the People's Court shall permit such request. If the lessor requests the dissolution of the financial leasing contract and the repossession of the leased property, and the lessee, by way of defense or counterclaim, advocates the return of the portion of the value of the leased property that exceeds the arrears of rent and other expenses, the People's Court shall handle such matters together. Where the parties dispute the value of the leased property, the value thereof shall be determined in accordance with the following rules:
(1) if the financial leasing contract provides therefor, in accordance with such provision;
(2) if the financial leasing contract does not provide therefor or the provision is unclear, in accordance with the agreed depreciation of the leased property and the residual value of the leased property after the expiration of the contract;
(3) if the value is still difficult to determine according to the methods provided in the preceding two subparagraphs, or if a party believes that the value determined according to the methods provided in the preceding two subparagraphs seriously deviates from the actual value of the leased property, a qualified institution shall be entrusted for appraisal upon a party's application.
